Q: How to get the cursor position in bash? A: <p>I don't understand the &quot;dirty&quot; method, but I do understand the problem of redefining the output stream via a $() call to capture the output of the function.</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>GetXY() {
  if [[ -t 0 ]]; then
    &gt;&amp;0 IFS=';' read -rsdR -p $'\e[6n' zY zX
    printf '%s %s' &quot;%zX&quot; &quot;${zY:2}&quot;
  fi
}
</code></pre>
<p>This cleanly reports the X and Y cursor position ready for the caller to read via a bash $(GetXY) which reassigns fd 1 (stdout) because the read statement's prompt is directed to fd 0 (stdin).</p>

Q: Subtract two timespec objects; find difference in time or duration A: <p>If microseconds is enough, it's easier to downgrade timespec to timeval using library functions.</p>
<pre><code>#include&lt;time.h&gt;
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include &lt;sys/time.h&gt;
//...

struct timespec before, after;
int rtc = clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &amp;before);
// do busy task here
rtc |= clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &amp;after);
if (rtc == 0)
{
    struct timeval b, a, diff;
    TIMESPEC_TO_TIMEVAL(&amp;a, &amp;after);
    TIMESPEC_TO_TIMEVAL(&amp;b, &amp;before);
    timersub(&amp;a, &amp;b, &amp;diff);
    fprintf(&quot;busy task completed in %ld.%06lds&quot;,
        diff.tv_sec, diff.tv_usec);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Wrap text in seaborn barplot x-axis python A: <p>Disc - This is my first post on 'StackOF'. Please excuse me if it is not up to your expectations. To wrap the x-axis labels in Seaborn is not possible (afaik) but even with Dexplot there are too many limitations. Here is the logic for achieving this using Matplotlib (using python).</p>
<pre><code>import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np

raw_data = {'plan_type':  ['this is big ticks number one', 'this is also a big ticks number two Group B', 'and this as well a very big ticks number three Group C', 'this is no exception than a very big ticks number four Group D','Group E'],
        'Total': [56,24,18,49,67],
        'Total_PY': [12,82,33,60,16]
}


df = pd.DataFrame(raw_data, 
              columns = ['plan_type', 'Total', 'Total_PY'])


fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(14,9))
ax = df.plot.bar(rot=0, ax=ax, color=['#FF5733','#2323ff'], 
             width = 0.7 )


for p in ax.patches[1:]:
h = p.get_height()
x = p.get_x()+p.get_width()/2.
if h != 0 :
    ax.annotate(&quot;%g&quot; % p.get_height(), xy=(x,h), xytext=(0,4), rotation=0, 
               textcoords=&quot;offset points&quot;, ha=&quot;center&quot;, va=&quot;bottom&quot;)


ax.set_xlim(-0.5, None)
ax.set_ylim(-0.5, 120)
ax.margins(y=0)

ax.legend(ncol = len(df.columns), loc=&quot;lower right&quot;, bbox_to_anchor=(0,1.02,1,0.08), 
          borderaxespad=0, title= 'Year' )


ax = plt.gca();
labels = df[&quot;plan_type&quot;].tolist()
new_labels = []

for tick in labels:
word_list = tick.split();
new_labels.append(word_list);


new_ticks = []
for label_tick in new_labels:

if(len(label_tick) &gt;= 2):

    joined = ' \n'.join(label_tick);
    new_ticks.append(joined) ;
else:
    joined = ' '.join(label_tick);
    new_ticks.append(joined) ;

plt.xticks(ticks = df.index, labels = new_ticks)
plt.xlabel('Type of Plan')
plt.ylabel('Count of Numbers')
</code></pre>
